MinGW runtime and compatibility libraries (binary artifacts when required)
  Windows builds made with GNU/MinGW carry the recursively detected non-system
  DLL closure beside the extension or Stata plugin. GCC runtimes can include
  libgcc, libstdc++, libgomp, libatomic, libquadmath, and libssp. Additional
  compatibility libraries can include libwinpthread and libdl. Windows runtime
  DLLs are copied byte-identically from the compiler provider; the release
  ledger records both hashes and rejects a mismatch.

  Linux wheels repaired by auditwheel may likewise carry a private GNU runtime
  library needed by the extension. auditwheel can rewrite ELF metadata or the
  SONAME while grafting that library, so byte identity is not claimed there.
  The release ledger separately records the provider path/hash, packaged member
  path/hash, package NEVRA, SOURCERPM, and exact source-RPM payload.

  GCC runtime libraries are distributed under GPLv3 with the GCC Runtime
  Library Exception 3.1 where stated by GCC. Verbatim license texts ship as:
    third_party/licenses/GCC-13.2.0-COPYING3
    third_party/licenses/GCC-13.2.0-COPYING.RUNTIME
  Versioned upstream information:
    https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/libstdc++/manual/license.html
    https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gcc-exception-3.1.html

  The exact corresponding sources and build recipes for redistributed GNU /
  MinGW runtime binaries are supplied in the versioned release asset:
    https://github.com/reisportela/xhdfe-xfe/releases/download/v2.24.2.20260822/xhdfe-2.24.2.20260822-corresponding-source.zip
  That asset includes a machine-readable provenance manifest and SHA-256
  manifest over the source archives actually supplied to the release job.

